    <body>
      <sec id="article-141824.s1" sec-type="Definition/Introduction">
        <title>Definition/Introduction</title>
        <p content-type="pubmed-excerpt">The Occupational Safety and Health Administration (OSHA) is a division of the United States Department of Labor responsible for ensuring workplace safety. OSHA develops and enforces standards designed to reduce occupational health risks and provides safety education and training for workers. Although these regulations apply broadly to all workplaces, additional requirements exist for high-risk occupations, including healthcare settings. These specialized standards address hazards such as bloodborne pathogens, hazardous chemicals, infectious diseases, and the proper use of personal protective equipment (PPE) to minimize exposure to dangerous materials. &#x000a0;</p>
        <p>An important aspect of workplace safety addressed by OSHA is the management and communication of chemical hazards.&#x000a0;Chemicals are widely used in product design, development, prototyping, and manufacturing, as well as in routine industrial applications such as disinfection and equipment maintenance. Although many chemicals pose minimal risk when handled appropriately, others may be highly hazardous and may cause serious injury or death if exposure occurs.</p>
        <p>To address these risks, OSHA established the Hazard Communication Standard (HCS), which provides a standardized system for communicating the hazards associated with chemical exposure in the workplace. The HCS aligns with the internationally recognized Globally Harmonized System of Classification and Labeling of Chemicals (GHS). Initially implemented in 1983, the standard underwent substantial revisions in 2012 to align with GHS Revision 3. OSHA later issued a final rule on May 20, 2024, effective July 19, 2024, updating the HCS to align primarily with GHS Revision 7. This update addresses implementation challenges from the 2012 revision and improves consistency with standards used by other US agencies and international trading partners. The overarching goal of HCS is to ensure that workers in any industry can readily identify chemical containers and understand the associated hazards.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141824.r1">[1]</xref> &#x000a0;</p>
        <p>OSHA enforces these regulations for chemical manufacturers and importers, requiring them to evaluate chemical hazards before production and to communicate those hazards to downstream users. Workplaces that use these chemicals must maintain proper HCS labeling on chemical containers, ensure that safety data sheets are readily accessible to employees, and provide appropriate education and training on chemical hazards. Under Standard 29 CFR 1910.1200, OSHA establishes the framework for chemical hazard classification and communication, helping reduce occupational exposure to harmful substances. By implementing these requirements and guidelines, OSHA holds employers accountable for meeting minimum safety standards and helps protect workers, particularly in high-risk industries.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-141824.s2" sec-type="Issues of Concern">
        <title>Issues of Concern</title>
        <p>According to the United States Bureau of Labor Statistics Census of Fatal Occupational Injuries, 5070 fatal work injuries occurred in the United States in 2024, representing a 4% decrease from 5283 fatalities in 2023. Additionally, the fatal work injury rate declined for the second year in a row to 3.3 deaths per 100,000 full-time equivalent workers in 2024. Transportation and material-moving occupations remain the occupational group with the highest number of fatalities.[U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics. <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.bls.gov/news.release/pdf/cfoi.pdf">National Census of Fatal Occupational Injuries in 2024</ext-link>] Despite these improvements, millions of nonfatal workplace injuries still occur each year, underscoring the continued public health importance of workplace safety regulations, including chemical hazard standards.[AFL-CIO. <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://aflcio.org/reports/dotj-2025">Death on the Job: The Toll of Neglect, 2025</ext-link>]</p>
        <p>Chemicals are used daily in both low- and high-risk industries as cleaning agents, raw materials for manufacturing, and components in the formulation or activation of other chemical products. Thousands of chemicals may be present in workplaces across the United States. The potential exposure risks associated with these substances are categorized under the GHS into 16 physical hazards, 10 health hazards, and 1 environmental hazard. Physical hazards include the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Oxidizing solids</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Liquids and gases</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Flammable solids, liquids, gases, and aerosols</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Explosives</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Desensitized explosives (a hazard class added in the 2024 Hazard Communication Standard update)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Gases under pressure</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Self-reactive substances</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Self-heating substances</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Pyrophoric solids and liquids</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Substances that emit flammable gases when in contact with water</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Organic peroxides</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Corrosive substances that damage metals</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Chemicals under pressure [Occupational Safety and Health Administration. <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.osha.gov/laws-regs/federalregister/2024-05-20">Hazard Communication Standard; Final Rule&#x000a0;(2024)</ext-link>]</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Health hazards include the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Acute toxicity</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Specific target organ toxicity from single or repeated exposure</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Skin corrosion or irritation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Serious eye damage or irritation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Respiratory and skin sensitization</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Aspiration hazards</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Germ cell mutagenicity</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Reproductive toxicity</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Carcinogenicity</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>The single environmental hazard category addresses chemicals that pose acute or chronic environmental toxicity.</p>
        <p>Each chemical container must display standardized labeling with pictograms and hazard information indicating the specific risks associated with exposure. In addition to labeling requirements, OSHA mandates that a safety data sheet accompany hazardous chemicals. The safety data sheet should provide the following detailed information:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Identification</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Hazard identification</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Composition and ingredient information</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>First-aid measures</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Firefighting measures</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Accidental release procedures</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Handling and storage recommendations</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Exposure controls and PPE</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Physical and chemical properties</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Stability and reactivity</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Toxicological and ecological information</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Disposal considerations</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Transportation details</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Applicable regulatory information <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141824.r2">[2]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>The OSHA HCS ensures that employees understand the hazards associated with the chemicals they may encounter in the workplace. To comply with the HCS, employers must implement several key requirements:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Inform employees about hazardous chemicals present in the workplace, including routine and accidental exposures, and the associated health and physical hazards.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Provide training for employees who handle hazardous chemicals, including initial training upon hire and refresher training whenever new chemical hazards are introduced.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Train employees on protective measures and safe handling procedures designed to minimize exposure.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Develop and maintain a written hazard communication program outlining how chemical hazards are communicated in the workplace.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Maintain accessible safety data sheets for each hazardous chemical.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Maintain an on-site inventory of hazardous chemicals and ensure that each container is labeled appropriately in English with required hazard information.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Historically, the OSHA HCS relied largely on performance-based requirements. The 2012 revision introduced a standardized labeling system, a 16-section safety data sheet format, and updated hazard classification criteria aligned with the GHS. The 2024 update improves the clarity and consistency of hazard communication for workers and first responders by revising criteria for certain health and physical hazard classifications, updating labeling requirements, including provisions for small containers, clarifying trade secret protections, and making technical amendments to safety data sheet content. The rule also updates classification criteria for aerosols, desensitized explosives, and flammable gases, and introduces a new hazard category, chemicals under pressure, within the aerosol class. OSHA estimates that these changes affect approximately 94% of safety data sheets and 64% of shipped container labels across regulated industries. The updated rule allows abbreviated label information for containers of 100 mL or less, with additional accommodations for even smaller containers, while ensuring that trade secret protections do not limit access to essential hazard information for workers and emergency responders.</p>
        <p>The rule includes a phased compliance schedule. Manufacturers, importers, and distributors of substances must comply by May 19, 2026, whereas employers must update workplace labeling, written hazard communication programs, and employee training by November 20, 2026. For chemical mixtures, manufacturer compliance is required by November 19, 2027, and employer compliance by May 19, 2028. During the transition period, regulated entities may comply with either the 2012 HCS, the updated 2024 standard, or both.[Occupational Safety and Health Administration.&#x000a0;<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.osha.gov/laws-regs/federalregister/2024-05-20">Hazard Communication Standard; Final Rule&#x000a0;(2024)</ext-link>]</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-141824.s3" sec-type="Clinical Significance">
        <title>Clinical Significance</title>
        <p>Because workers may encounter a wide range of chemicals in occupational settings, identifying the specific agent responsible for an exposure and determining an appropriate treatment strategy can be challenging.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141824.r3">[3]</xref> One analysis examining 12 professions demonstrated the scope of this issue, identifying as many as 685 disinfectants not intended for human use containing 152 active ingredients, along with 763 insecticides containing 97 active ingredients currently used in industry.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141824.r4">[4]</xref> Additional studies report occupational exposures involving 200 to 308 chemicals across multiple professions.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141824.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141824.r6">[6]</xref> Given this diversity of potential exposures, obtaining a rapid but thorough history from the patient or witnesses is essential to help narrow the list of possible agents and guide diagnostic evaluation and treatment.</p>
        <p>Previous analyses have identified corrosive liquids and flammable gases as among the most common chemical exposures, many of which occur in workplace settings.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141824.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;Frequently implicated agents include propane-butane, hydrochloric acid, sulfuric acid, methane, hydroxides, ammonia, and butyric acid. Although this list is not exhaustive, these substances represent some of the most commonly encountered chemical hazards and should be considered prominently in the differential diagnosis when the specific exposure is initially unknown.</p>
        <p>In healthcare settings, occupational chemical exposures most commonly involve cleaning and sterilizing agents; disinfectants; detergents; solvents; heavy metals; certain medications, such as antineoplastic or antibiotic agents; anesthetic gases; and laboratory chemicals, such as formaldehyde and xylene. Exposure risks vary by job responsibilities, so chemicals routinely encountered in a worker's role should be prioritized in the differential diagnosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141824.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141824.r8">[8]</xref>&#x000a0;In addition, similar to the monitoring required for employees working with radiation sources such as fluoroscopy, healthcare facilities should implement strategies to monitor both individual exposure and ambient chemical levels to help reduce cumulative occupational exposure.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141824.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141824.r9">[9]</xref>&#x000a0;Secondary exposure is another important consideration when treating patients exposed to hazardous chemicals. An analysis of more than 120,000 chemical incidents identified only 9 cases of secondary exposure among hospital personnel during treatment of chemically exposed patients, with inhalation representing the most common mechanism. The investigators concluded that standard PPE, including gloves and gowns, is generally sufficient to protect healthcare personnel, with additional specialized protection typically required only when managing exposures to highly toxic agents such as sarin.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141824.r10">[10]</xref></p>
        <p>In addition to PPE and monitoring, education and training remain among the most effective strategies for reducing occupational chemical exposure.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141824.r11">[11]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141824.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141824.r13">[13]</xref>&#x000a0;Although training is required under OSHA regulations, ongoing training is particularly important to reinforce knowledge of hazards that may not be encountered regularly in daily practice. A survey of more than 12,000 hospital employees found that chemical-specific training for handling high-risk substances, such as antineoplastic agents, aerosolized medications, strong disinfectants and sterilants, and surgical smoke, was incomplete, with reported training rates as low as 52% for aerosolized antibiotics and 57% for surgical smoke exposure.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141824.r14">[14]</xref></p>
        <p>The same survey also revealed that only 32% of respondents reported that their employer had procedures to reduce exposure to surgical smoke, whereas 56% reported that their employer had procedures to reduce exposure to anesthetic gases.&#x000a0;These findings highlight ongoing gaps in workplace safety practices. Healthcare facilities should also recognize that the 2024 update to the OSHA HCS introduces revised training requirements for updated hazard classifications, labeling for small containers, and changes to safety data sheet content. Employers must ensure that employee training programs are updated and implemented in accordance with the phased compliance deadlines established by the revised rule.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141824.r15">[15]</xref>[Occupational Safety and Health Administration.&#x000a0;<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.osha.gov/laws-regs/federalregister/2024-05-20">Hazard Communication Standard; Final Rule&#x000a0;(2024)</ext-link>]</p>
        <p>Finally, the potential for long-term health consequences is one of the most underrecognized aspects of hazardous chemical exposure. Individuals exposed to hazardous chemicals may initially remain asymptomatic and may not seek further evaluation. However, certain conditions, such as malignancy, may develop years after initial exposure, depending on the nature, intensity, and duration of chemical exposure. For individuals who experience acute exposures, such as accidental spills or inhalation events, long-term monitoring may be necessary to detect delayed health effects.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141824.r16">[16]</xref> Efforts should also be made to minimize further exposure, as repeated or ongoing contact with hazardous chemicals may worsen or accelerate the development of associated disease processes.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-141824.s4" sec-type="Nursing, Allied Health, and Interprofessional Team Interventions">
        <title>Nursing, Allied Health, and Interprofessional Team Interventions</title>
        <p>OSHA establishes regulations designed to reduce occupational injuries and illnesses by promoting safe workplace practices. The HCS is one&#x000a0;of OSHA's key frameworks for chemical exposure and requires employers to identify and communicate the hazards associated with workplace chemicals. The HCS aligns with the GHS to ensure standardized labeling, pictograms, and safety information across industries. Under this system, chemical manufacturers and employers must classify chemical hazards, label containers appropriately, maintain safety data sheets, and provide employee education and training regarding chemical risks and protective measures. These requirements are intended to ensure that workers can quickly recognize chemical hazards, understand associated risks, and take appropriate steps to minimize exposure and prevent injury. &#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Effective management of chemical exposures in healthcare settings requires coordinated interprofessional collaboration to ensure patient-centered care and optimize safety outcomes. Clinicians and advanced practice providers play a central role in identifying potential chemical exposures through careful history-taking, clinical evaluation, and diagnostic decision-making, while coordinating treatment strategies and determining the need for specialty consultation, toxicology input, or long-term monitoring. Nurses contribute by performing ongoing patient assessments, implementing decontamination and supportive care measures, monitoring for complications, and reinforcing patient education regarding exposure risks and prevention. Pharmacists provide expertise in medication safety and toxicology, assisting with antidote selection, medication management, and evaluation of potential drug-chemical interactions. Other healthcare professionals, including occupational health specialists, respiratory therapists, environmental safety personnel, and infection control teams, contribute by identifying exposure sources, implementing environmental controls, and ensuring appropriate PPE use.&#x000a0;&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Clear communication, role delineation, and coordinated care planning among team members enhance patient safety and improve outcomes for individuals exposed to hazardous chemicals. Interprofessional collaboration enables rapid recognition of exposure risks, timely implementation of protective measures for healthcare personnel, and the development of comprehensive management plans that address both acute treatment and, when necessary, long-term monitoring. Through effective teamwork, standardized communication strategies, and adherence to OSHA safety principles, healthcare professionals can reduce secondary exposures, support recovery, and promote safer workplace environments while maintaining high standards of patient-centered care.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-141824.s5" sec-type="Nursing, Allied Health, and Interprofessional Team Monitoring">
        <title>Nursing, Allied Health, and Interprofessional Team Monitoring</title>
        <p>The hospital and healthcare team should actively promote chemical safety and educate employees and patients about the risks of chemical exposure. Nurses, occupational health personnel, pharmacists, environmental safety specialists, and other allied health professionals play an important role in monitoring for potential chemical hazards, reinforcing safety protocols, and identifying signs of exposure among staff and patients.</p>
        <p>Proper signage, clear warning labels on hazardous chemical containers, and consistent use of appropriate PPE help protect healthcare professionals and reduce the risk of occupational injury. Educational strategies should incorporate standardized pictograms and hazard labels that clearly communicate the specific risks associated with each chemical, allowing healthcare personnel to quickly recognize potential dangers and implement appropriate safety measures. The use of these visual hazard indicators has proven highly effective, estimated to have prevented between 107,000 and 459,000 acute chemical injuries and contributed to reductions in carcinogen exposure. Although chemical hazards remain a significant occupational risk, proper education, safety precautions, and standardized labeling have substantially reduced both acute exposures and long-term health complications.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Interprofessional collaboration is essential for maintaining a safe clinical environment and ensuring regulatory compliance. With the 2024 HCS final rule now in effect and phased compliance deadlines extending through 2028, healthcare administrators, occupational health nurses, and safety officers should proactively review facility chemical inventories, verify that updated safety data sheets have been received from suppliers, update written hazard communication programs, and ensure that staff training reflects the updated hazard classification criteria and labeling requirements under GHS Revision 7. Ongoing communication among clinicians, nurses, pharmacists, environmental safety personnel, and hospital leadership helps ensure the timely identification of hazards, adherence to safety protocols, and coordinated responses to chemical exposures, ultimately improving patient and workplace safety and overall team performance. &#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
